Subject: Weak references of objects live in a runtime table, not in the object

What this does
  - quickjs.c: JSObject loses `first_weak_ref` (8 bytes); an object keeps one bit, `has_weak_ref`, next to `values_inline` (now a bit as well), and the list head of an object that has weak references is kept in a table in the runtime (open addressing keyed by the object, entries removed by shifting). The lookups that returned the address of the field return the address of the table slot; a record that leaves an object's list releases the entry when the list is empty; freeing an object takes its list out of the table and resets it. sizeof(JSObject) goes from 72 to 64 bytes and the union that holds the class data moves from offset 48 to 40, so a plain object with two inline slots is 96 bytes in the block instead of 104.

Why we need it
  Every object paid 8 bytes for a list that nearly none have. The units read the field offsets from the engine's layout, so nothing in generated code changes but the numbers.

Soundness
  The bit is the only thing the free paths and the young-object fast paths test, as the pointer was. Strings and symbols keep their own list head. The table holds no reference to its keys: an entry exists exactly while the object has weak records, and the object's free removes it before the memory is reused.

Failure modes
  The table grows by doubling and aborts if it cannot allocate, as a weak record that cannot be added cannot be dropped silently.

